      ":question/worked-solution": "First, let $u=9x+12.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{9x+12}\\,\\text{d}x & =9\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{d}u\\right) \\\\ & =1\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\ & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|9x+12|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
